#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b591b959-0fa6-5a9f-9c27-2d53c19f3ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150577} {:answer/id #uuid "d6492c8b-6cd1-5aa0-99f1-a1b0cdabe68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150578} {:answer/id #uuid "feb74112-2dcd-5dc5-9b05-85e93ac4fe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150579} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4b28d0-0159-54f3-bdb2-0d6d0d566c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150580} {:answer/id #uuid "69563d64-d0ff-507a-b25a-bf11edff79ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b591b959-0fa6-5a9f-9c27-2d53c19f3ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150577} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6f6f0a-f8bc-5eb3-93b0-03987e8b9e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be3693e3-b291-55cc-9e6f-d294ae6aac43" :question/math-academy-id "q-6881" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97aba8b1-4d57-59f1-8681-27a6f5ef87df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\cos \\theta  + 3, y = 6\\sin \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151213} {:answer/id #uuid "c45d0cc5-7ee8-542f-807b-60210f8560ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\sin \\theta  + 3, y = 6\\cos \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151214} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ed8c91-be29-513e-b16d-df6bd83fd9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  - 3, y = \\sin \\theta  + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151215} {:answer/id #uuid "094d6df1-4230-52c5-a868-37f8f1c16aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\sin \\theta  + 3, y = 3\\cos \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151216} {:answer/id #uuid "576033fc-0922-5555-a8fc-419643c57fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos \\theta  + 3, y = 3\\sin \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576033fc-0922-5555-a8fc-419643c57fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos \\theta  + 3, y = 3\\sin \\theta  - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151217} :answer-field/id #uuid "85479bab-2698-5739-baf8-d6800ff00faa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c09e84a-e7d0-5c50-95f2-01ada1064320" :question/math-academy-id "q-78680" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d083840a-941f-529b-92bc-a21417ee64af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366174} {:answer/id #uuid "68d6142e-0b01-5ece-8ab8-4e0cd772b887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366175} {:answer/id #uuid "348d0679-80cc-51a3-844c-3795fddf01c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366176} {:answer/id #uuid "46b9267c-93a3-503b-a07f-904d0aa1048a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366177} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb54a88-8eb5-5849-a2ec-91249798d06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68d6142e-0b01-5ece-8ab8-4e0cd772b887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366175} :answer-field/id #uuid "3990cf93-b107-596e-989a-e69158aa996d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9d06d5a-1710-506f-9441-28c4dc1ef4cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79273" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/48c5190fc4f45f4bf3e76a56977339559ceb30ca90a41411d3bc4df2362b0787.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/546b9f72124a7eb05877db3600e40b5772404a1a3378de67cfbe3320c78ac2ba.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63d32c88-76e2-5390-82c6-9eb900a34c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366181} {:answer/id #uuid "9c04c4a0-2411-5cd3-a749-0d949b6aed42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366182} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3a4619-1ca2-528b-920a-f4767aa1db0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366183} {:answer/id #uuid "7a077b25-808c-5e1f-8296-4b190dd6f6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366184} {:answer/id #uuid "13435a9e-8fec-5dbb-add7-b89405f650a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c04c4a0-2411-5cd3-a749-0d949b6aed42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366182} :answer-field/id #uuid "f735d59c-2e8a-5e2f-bfbe-439b61b0d80d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9082f86b-7c0f-5dd7-b074-5c0e7d067128" :question/math-academy-id "q-79287" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c0d8db5-38cc-5572-a53a-4584905f8b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366188} {:answer/id #uuid "a88347c4-eb37-5e1b-ba92-52d2e2b28c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366189} {:answer/id #uuid "1c46b20b-d7fa-5576-ae03-0355b8965214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366190} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ce2998-0398-5a1e-92b2-b2d83a4c7a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366191} {:answer/id #uuid "5869fe13-1f93-5842-b33f-b20908c60d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7ce2998-0398-5a1e-92b2-b2d83a4c7a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366191} :answer-field/id #uuid "daebd331-accc-5e35-b808-aeaa31338f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7905e0ad-e4b9-544c-af54-d96f3b36bc86" :question/math-academy-id "q-79377" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{x+5}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$"}]}
